  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a magnetogenetic-style membrane system in which an external electromagnetic wave is proposed to influence an engineered ion channel through a ferritin-based attachment. A cell membrane is shown with a modified TRPV1 channel spanning the lipid bilayer. On the cytoplasmic side, the channel is linked to a GFP–ferritin cluster through an anti-GFP camelid nanobody, creating a physical connection between ferritin and the channel.\n\nRed wave lines indicate the incoming electromagnetic field interacting near the ferritin complex. The main idea appears to be that this stimulation alters the modified TRPV1 channel, leading it to open. An inward arrow through the channel suggests ion entry across the membrane. The labeled ions around the channel are Ca2+ and Na+, indicating that activation permits these cations to pass into the cell.\n\nOverall, the image presents a schematic mechanism for remote control of cellular activity: electromagnetic stimulation acts on ferritin tethered to an engineered TRPV1 channel, which then changes conformation and allows calcium and sodium influx. The figure emphasizes the designed molecular coupling and the resulting ion transport rather than quantitative data.",
